{"content":"🚨 **IMPORTANT: a watchdog-killed attempt's late response is silently dropped — retrying multiplies the abandoned-socket ceiling**

`_sendWatchedOnce` races `httpClient.send` against a `.timeout()`. When the connect watchdog fires, the underlying request keeps running (package:http cannot abort a pending send). Two cases:

1. **Endpoint never answers** — the half-open socket is held by the shared keep-alive client (`sharedProviderHttpClient` is never closed) until process end. Unfixable without a cancellable send; pre-existing ceiling, documented for the fetch path.
2. **Endpoint answers late** (slow-but-alive, e.g. a reasoning endpoint that takes >180s to first byte) — the late `StreamedResponse` is dropped unreferenced by `Future.timeout`: the socket is never drained back to the keep-alive pool **and** the provider may have started generating (and billing) a generation whose answer is thrown away.

Before this PR, one stalled turn abandoned 1 request. The new retry loop (line 582) turns that into up to `providerConnectRetries + 1` = 3 abandoned requests per stalled turn, in exactly the failure mode this PR targets. Note `sendProviderFetch` documents this ceiling in its doc comment, but the streaming retry path doesn't address or document the accumulation.

Suggested mitigation for case 2 — let `_sendWatchedOnce` expose the pending future (e.g. an `onPending` callback) and, when deciding to retry, attach a janitor:

```dart
// in _sendWatched, after deciding to retry:
pendingResponse?.then((r) {
  // a late answer is dropped: release the socket (and make the
  // orphaned generation visible in fa.log instead of silent)
  r.stream.drain<void>().catchError((_) {});
}, onError: (_) {});
```

Zero bytes received keeps the "nothing double-billed" claim true for case 1; case 2 is worth at least a drain + a `[net]` note so silent generation loss is observable.
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment1.md"}

{"content":"⚠️ **SUGGESTION: `chatgpt-codex` bypasses the retry choke point — the PR description overstates coverage**

The PR description calls `_sendWatched` "the single choke point all streaming adapters route through", but `_ChatGptCodexSession._request` (this hunk) sends directly via `client.send(...).timeout(...)`:

- a codex connect stall gets the **watchdog but not the retry** — it escalates to `TransientRetryStream`/roles failover, consuming the *shared* 3-attempt transient budget instead of the dedicated connect budget every other adapter now enjoys (copilot/dial are client decorators, so they do route through `_sendWatched` — codex is the only true bypass);
- unlike `_sendWatchedOnce` (and `createSseIterator`), this send doesn't race `cancelToken.onCancel` — a user abort during a wedged connect now waits up to the full 180s watchdog instead of propagating promptly.

Behavior remains safe (the rendered `TimeoutException:` text still classifies and fails over — pinned by your tests), so this is a coverage/consistency gap, not a bug. Worth either routing the codex send through a shared helper or recording a follow-up issue so the asymmetry is deliberate.
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment3.md"}

{"content":"⚠️ **SUGGESTION: `_consumeSseLines` reimplements the SSE consumption loop without the cancel-token wiring `createSseIterator` has**

`createSseIterator(response, cancelToken, …)` attaches `cancelToken.onCancel → abandon + iterator.cancel()`, so a user abort detaches the byte pipeline immediately, even mid-silence. This new loop only checks `cancelToken?.throwIfCancelled()` after a line arrives (line 308), so an abort during a silent stretch is honored at the next line or at the idle watchdog — up to `effectiveProviderStreamIdleTimeout` (5 min default) later.

Pre-existing behavior was the same (and unbounded), so this is not a regression — the idle watchdog actually bounds it for the first time. But since this method is written fresh in this PR, wiring the token would give codex the same prompt abort latency as every other adapter:

```dart
if (cancelToken != null) {
  unawaited(cancelToken.onCancel.then((_) async {
    await lines.cancel().catchError((_) {});
  }));
}
```

(the `finally` cancel stays as the backstop).
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment4.md"}

{"content":"⚠️ **SUGGESTION: pin the "identical payload re-sent" claim — the retried request's body/headers are never asserted**

The PR description's safety argument leans on "the identical payload is simply re-sent" via `_reissue`. `_StallClient` records the requests but only asserts `hasLength(3)`; a `_reissue` regression that dropped `bodyBytes` or an auth header would still pass — every attempt returns 200 from the mock regardless.

A cheap pin inside this test:

```dart
expect(client.requests[1].bodyBytes, client.requests[0].bodyBytes);
expect(
  client.requests[1].headers['content-type'],
  client.requests[0].headers['content-type'],
);
```

(The redirect path has coverage elsewhere, but this is the first retry consumer of `_reissue` with a *sent* request as the clone source — worth pinning at the new call site, line 579 of provider_common.dart.)

Minor adjacent gap: the CLI `_fail` branch (bin/fah.dart) and the app's degrade branch (agent_service.dart `on ConfigException`) of the `FA_PROVIDER_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` fold are untested — only the happy fold is.
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment5.md"}

